    {
      "filename": "Central Hall, Keighley.jpg",
      "title": "File:Central Hall, Keighley.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2c/Central_Hall%2C_Keighley.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Central_Hall,_Keighley.jpg",
      "width": 829,
      "height": 1024,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0",
      "artist": "TJBlackwell",
      "credit": "Own work",
      "source": "Central Hall, Keighley",
      "description": "Built in 1893, Central Hall is a former Methodist Chapel on Alice Street. It became a space for the Keighley community in 1948 and was used for all kinds of activities over the years before being closed down amid a state of dereliction in 2003. Its resurrection was carried out by   Hodson Architects, under the project management of KIVCA, an infrastructure support organisation who has led a project funded by Bradford Council to renovate the old building into a new community resource centre.\nThe original-resolution copy of this photograph can be found online here.",
      "dateCreated": "2011-01-18",
      "sourceType": "core",
      "sourceArticleId": "keighley-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "gcwd",
      "smallPath": "_m/g/c/w/d/keighley-wp/built-1893-central-hal-small.webp"
    },
    {
      "filename": "Cavendish Street, Keighley.jpg",
      "title": "File:Cavendish Street, Keighley.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/64/Cavendish_Street%2C_Keighley.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cavendish_Street,_Keighley.jpg",
      "width": 1024,
      "height": 872,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0",
      "artist": "TJBlackwell",
      "credit": "Own work",
      "source": "Cavendish Street, Keighley",
      "description": "Once upon a time, Cavendish Street was merely a narrow footpath cutting across an old town field, which was widened and redeveloped in the late 19th century. It eventually became a grand Victorian shopping street, the facades of which were intended to impress visitors as they arrived from the new Keighley train station, opened in 1883. At this time, the town was one of the busiest and wealthiest in Yorkshire. All of the new buildings were ornate and worthy of a town of such wealth and prestige as a result of its industrial might, and reflect the strong sense of civic pride that had developed in the area.\nThis image can be found in its original resolution online in this set of images.",
      "dateCreated": "2011-01-05",
      "sourceType": "core",
      "sourceArticleId": "keighley-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "gcwd",
      "smallPath": "_m/g/c/w/d/keighley-wp/once-upon-time-cavendi-small.webp"
    },
    {
      "filename": "Former Mechanics' Institute, Keighley.jpg",
      "title": "File:Former Mechanics' Institute, Keighley.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ac/Former_Mechanics%27_Institute%2C_Keighley.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Former_Mechanics%27_Institute,_Keighley.jpg",
      "width": 1024,
      "height": 683,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0",
      "artist": "TJBlackwell",
      "credit": "Own work",
      "source": "Former Mechanics' Institute, Keighley",
      "description": "These old buildings once belonged to Keighley College, but the institution recently moved to a more modern property so the structure on the left is demolished, and the right is scheduled to be demolished. Designed by Lockwood and Mawson - architects of many of the grandest structures in Bradford - the building on the left of this photo originally housed the Mechanics Institute and is an important component of Lord Street. It dates variously from 1887, 1914 and 1932 and is constructed of dressed sandstone with a blue slate roof. Keighley, whilst it has more than a fair share of concrete, retains a lot of northern heritage from a tapestry of beautiful old Victorian buildings which are liberally scattered throughout the town centre. This photograph can be found in its original resolution online in this set of images.",
      "dateCreated": "2011-01-31",
      "sourceType": "core",
      "sourceArticleId": "keighley-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "gcwd",
      "smallPath": "_m/g/c/w/d/keighley-wp/these-old-buildings-on-small.webp"
    },
    {
